WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i fell in love  with running track and field when i was  13 during my freshman year of high  school  running became my answer my identity my  anesthesia  when things at home were tough i ran  when school stressed me out i ran  i loved lacing up my shoes my track  spikes were super fly  all white a zipper that covered the  laces which i thought made me more  aerodynamic  and dusted with a neon green saucony  logo  when i wanted to feel free i laced up  those bad boys  and i ran and i ran and i ran  turns out i didn't just have a love for  running  i had a gift for it too  i arrived at stanford with a plan to  walk onto the track team and run my  favorite event  the 400. but my speed caught the eye of  stanford's rugby team which catapulted  me into the world of division 1  athletics  rugby was running but now with kicking  catching  passing and friends as a 17 year old  undergraduate freshman  i competed in my first national  championship  sounds pretty surreal for a girl from  sacramento who just started running to  feel free  i wish i could tell you that that love  never faded  and that sports love me back but i can't  i'm here to tell the truth i'm here to  break silence  i'm here to call out high school and  college sports programs  from coaches to athletic directors from  bystanders to fans  for two often valuing athletes for their  statistics  without any regard for their physical  and psychological  well-being i'm asking you to listen to  my story  to my friend zach's story and to so many  other stories  i'm asking you to sit with the  discomfort of learning the price  that so many athletes pay for your  entertainment  the physical demands of a decade of  competitive sports were enough to break  me  i was 16 when i stopped having periods i  was assured that all was well  and they would return when i stopped  running and gained weight  and then the injury started playing  through them was  normal and expected i played rugby with  a broken hand  i played with frequent shoulder  dislocations  by the time our game schedule allowed  for surgery  my shoulder popped out of its socket  with every sneeze  facial fractures concern for a detached  retina more ankle sprains than i can  count  season ending concussion i could go on  and yet the psychological suffering was  more dizzying  i lost what felt like my soul identity  and became  unhealthily conscious of the foods i put  in my body  but one story hurts more than any other  during my junior year of college i lost  one of the most important people in my  life  grandma fry grandma fry was my great  grandmother and she was the only  grandparent that i ever knew  this was and still is the biggest loss  i have experienced about a week into my  departure to attend her funeral  matt my coach called to ask when i was  returning  he said that he could excuse miss  practices up to this point  but if i expected to start it was time  to come back  it was okay if i wasn't ready but if i  wanted to keep my spot as a starter  it was time to return it may sound like  matt was giving me a choice  but he knew that i idolized him he knew  that this 20 year old still had dreams  of making the national team  he wasn't giving me a choice he gave me  an ultimatum  i don't fully blame him coaches are not  paid to prioritize us  as students as people or as  post-collegiate professionals  we are assets and they are compensated  for winning  but try to imagine what it was like for  this 20 year old  me to be akin to a racehorse  propped up by medals posters with my  face plastered in gyms around campus and  on t-shirts  but unworthy of understanding compassion  and healing  if it costs matt the team or the system  anything even just a little more time  i'm ashamed to admit that i returned i  walked away from my family  in the midst of our shared grief to play  in a single season  game that didn't actually matter  it took me years to fully understand the  impact of that decision  on me and my family  but this isn't just about me so let me  tell you a story about my friend zach  who recently passed away  zach was a guy in college who everyone  wanted to be around he was tall  funny a baseball and football player  and a great dancer at parties i'd see  him whip out these insane dance moves  drawing everyone's eyes towards him  meanwhile  i would awkwardly sway nearby hoping  that no one could tell  how little rhythm i truly had  on campus on film in stats and on the  surface  zach was someone who seemingly had it  all over time however i saw him  transform from this  phenomenal carefree star athlete who  turned pro  to one who was comfortable vocalizing  elements of his story that broke from  this  perfect facade that he showed a perfect  facade that many of us showed  about a year before his death zack  shared in an interview how devastating  it was to medically retire from one too  many concussions  how he suffered through an identity  crisis while simultaneously shouldering  lyme disease mental health issues and  family trauma  in this interview zack admitted that he  didn't even like sports  he died of an accidental drug overdose  at 26.  i don't want to speculate on the  circumstances surrounding his death  and only zach truly knows what he was  going through  but i often wonder how sports played a  role  why he felt pressure to play a game he  didn't enjoy  and how my friend a lovely guy lost his  life  so early zach started to remove his mask  and in the process inspired me to take  off my own  but this isn't just about me and zack  it is so much bigger  it's been five years since i retired my  last pair of cleats  five years since i began to wrestle with  the physical  and emotional damage that a decade of  competitive sports  imprinted on me imprinted on us  so today is the first day that i  publicly break my silence surrounding my  experience  today i'm calling out a culture that  left me like so many athletes to  shoulder  the lifetime costs of these injuries  at best i have three periods a year and  i still don't know why  will i ever be able to have children  will i ever have a normal relationship  with my body  was any of this worth it  i feel really sad  and really angry  why didn't anyone protect me  why didn't anyone protect us  things need to change  that starts with athletes breaking their  silence  more importantly it starts with you  listening  just listen don't ask us to fix the  problem  sit with the heaviness the discomfort  center the athletes who've silently  carried it for so long  i'm scared that if you don't we walk out  of this room  and the same thing will happen to the  next 13 year old girl  who is falling in love with her track  spikes as we speak  i'm asking you to make space for us  to break this silence

